ker in round 1. We need to respond to Agent 2's point. We can either agree, challenge, or add nuance. We have our own notes. We should consider the evidence. We might agree with D but raise concerns about crew cooperation and adaptability, maybe ask for more info about other candidates. Or we could propose a different candidate. But we need to follow the process: we should make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing current discussion, using explicit facts, and state our vote.\n\nWe have our own notes: D takes responsibility seriously, solves problems extremely well, concentrates very well, responds adequately to unexpected events. Concerns: hot temper, know-it-all attitude. Also D is considered moody, regarded as a loner. Those are additional concerns not yet mentioned. We can bring those up. Also we have facts about other candidates: A quick correct decisions, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education; B very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, regarded as pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone; C can anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, unfriendly, eats unhealthily.\n\nWe could argue that while D has strong technical and responsibility traits, his hot temper and loner attitude may be more problematic for crew cooperation than the other candidates' issues. Perhaps B's stress handling and conscientiousness make him a strong alternative, despite communication tone issues. Or C's situational awareness is valuable. But we need to decide our vote. We could either stick with D, but raise concerns and ask for more info on crew cooperation for all candidates. Or we could shift to B as a better balanced candidate.\n\nGiven the criteria, crew cooperation and communication are crucial. D's hot temper and loner may be severe. B's \"sometimes adopts the wrong tone\" is also a communication issue, but B is very conscientious and handles stress well. A's egocentric and difficulty communicating are also severe. C's unfriendly and not open to new ideas also problematic.\n\nWhich candidate has the least severe crew cooperation concerns? D's hot temper and know-it-all may be severe, but B's wrong tone may be less severe? Hard to say. D also has \"moody\" and \"loner\". That suggests poor teamwork. B is \"pretentious\" and \"wrong tone\". Might be similar.\n\nWe could propose that we need more info on crew cooperation for each candidate before final decision. But we must give a vote. Perhaps we can vote for Candidate B as a provisional recommendation, arguing that his stress resilience and conscientiousness outweigh his communication tone issues, which may be more manageable than D's temperament.\n\nLet's decide: I will propose Candidate B as my current recommendation, challenging D's suitability due to crew cooperation concerns. Provide evidence: B handles stress well, very conscientious, good at weather assessment, but has communication tone issues. D has hot temper, know-it-all, moody, loner. Argue that crew cooperation is critical for long-distance flights, and D's issues may be more detrimental.\n\nThus vote for B.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 2's point: they recommended D. We'll say I am less convinced because of crew cooperation concerns.\n\nProvide explicit facts: D's hot temper, know-it-all, moody, loner; B's stress handling, conscientiousness, good weather assessment, but sometimes wrong tone.\n\nConclude that B is a stronger overall fit.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON."}
